// HelpAlerts — Alerts (generated from docs/shell/help-alerts.md).
const HelpAlerts = "\x1b[38;5;208mAlerts\x1b[0m\n  An alert evaluates a value from stdin against a condition.\n  When the condition holds, alert writes the alert message to stderr — in a\n  cron, that stderr drives incidents and notifications.\n  When the condition does not hold, stdin passes through to stdout\n  unchanged.\n  alert must be the last stage of a pipeline, and the last (or only)\n  command of a script.\n\n  If the pipeline ahead of alert fails (an unknown source, a bad stage),\n  the alert is not evaluated: the shell shows the error, and a cron run is\n  recorded with status error and opens no incident. Empty output from a\n  pipeline that ran (a grep with no match) counts as empty input, and\n  absent fires on it.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConditions (summary)\x1b[0m\n  - above <N> — value greater than N\n  - below <N> — value less than N\n  - eq <N>, neq <N>\n  - changed — value differs from previous run\n  - absent — no input received in the window\n  - rising <N>% — increase over baseline\n  - falling <N>% — decrease over baseline\n  - any — fires when stdin is non-empty. It is the default when no\n    condition is given.\n\n  See man alert for grammar, --duration, --average, --msg.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mComposite alerts (state across multiple crons)\x1b[0m\n  To fire on the combined state of more than one cron, compose\n  predicate builtins with bash && || ! inside a subshell, end it with\n  an echo, and pipe that into alert, which fires when anything comes out:\n\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  To combine conditions on the logs themselves, count both in one pass over\n  the cron's time range and alert on the result. This fires when errors\n  are above 50 and nothing came from the canary service:\n\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log /log/myco/prod/canary/main.log \\\n      | awk 'LEVEL==\"ERROR\"{e++} SERVICE==\"canary\"{c++} END{print (e>50 && c+0==0) ? 1 : 0}' \\\n      | alert above 0 --notify=critical\n\n  Keep row-limiting head -n / tail -n out of cron pipelines: they cap the\n  rows read and can silently truncate the window. (At the prompt, tail -n\n  500 | awk 'LEVEL==\"ERROR\"' | wc -l counts errors among the last 500\n  rows, while grep ERROR | tail -n 5 gives the last five matches.)\n\n  The predicate family — weft::is_above, weft::is_below,\n  weft::is_absent, weft::has_changed, weft::is_rising,\n  weft::is_falling, weft::is_outlier, weft::is_seasonal — each\n  exits 0/1 and has a Lua mirror (weft.is_above(...) etc.) that\n  returns true/false. See Alerting strategies for the full\n  catalogue plus seasonal/outlier guidance and Weft functions for the\n  per-function reference.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSingle-row and multi-row\x1b[0m\n  By default alert treats stdin as one scalar per tick. With\n  -k <field>, each non-blank line is one row and <field> is\n  the value to compare. With -t <field>, another field names\n  the row for per-tag incident dedup. <field> is either a\n  1-indexed whitespace column (-k 2) or a .-prefixed JSON\n  path (-k .latency_ms); -k and -t must agree on which form.\n\n    # per-host disk alert (positional)\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/metrics.log \\\n      | jq -r 'select(.type==\"disk\") | \"\\(.host) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # same alert against JSONL — no jq -r reformatting (jq -c keeps one row per line)\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/metrics.log | jq -c 'select(.type==\"disk\")' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n\x1b[38;5;208m/etc/notify.yaml\x1b[0m\n  --notify=<alias> looks up a destination from your notify.yaml\n  — a YAML map of alias name → per-service targets. Edit with:\n\n    vi /etc/notify.yaml\n\n  Each alias bundles one or more targets across four service types:\n  slack:, pagerduty:, webhook:, and email:. All are lists;\n  each entry is either a bare string or a mapping.\n\n    ops:\n      slack:\n        - \"#alerts\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - ops-daytime\n      webhook:\n        - \"https://hooks.example.com/weft/ops\"\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n\n    critical:\n      slack:\n        - \"#oncall\"\n        - \"#pager\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - name: primary\n          severity: critical\n      webhook:\n        - url: \"https://intake.example.com/alerts\"\n          headers:\n            X-Signing-Secret: \"change-me\"\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n        - \"lead@example.com\"\n\n  Slack — channel names (\"#ops\") or {channel: \"#name\"}.\n  PagerDuty — integration names (severity defaults to error),\n  or {name: \"...\", severity: \"...\"} (critical/error/warning/info).\n  Webhook — bare URL strings, or {url: \"...\", headers: {...}}.\n  POSTs a JSON payload with v (value 2), cron (the cron name), alias, ts_unix, body.\n  Header values are credentials: cat /etc/notify.yaml shows them as\n  \"<redacted>\". Leave a \"<redacted>\" in place to keep the stored value, or\n  write a new one to replace it.\n  Email — bare addresses, or {address: \"...\"}. Sends a plain-text\n  email with the cron name as subject (requires SMTP configuration).\n\n  Slack and PagerDuty credentials are configured with weft integration\n  (or under Account → Integrations in the browser); notify.yaml names\n  destinations:\n\n    weft integration slack connect\n    weft integration pagerduty add ops --key <integration-key>\n    weft integration list\n\n  The PagerDuty integration name must match one you've registered there.\n  Webhook and email destinations take their URLs and addresses per alias.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTesting an alias\x1b[0m\n    echo \"test fire\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n  Interactive runs dispatch immediately and open no incident\n  (incidents belong to crons).\n"

// HelpBuiltins — Builtins (generated from docs/shell/help-builtins.md).
const HelpBuiltins = "\x1b[38;5;208mQuerying logs\x1b[0m\n  - cat /log/<ns>/<env>/<svc>/main.log — stream all lines for a service (/log/<ns>/<svc>/<kind>.log reads it across every env)\n  - head /log/<ns>/<env>/<svc>/main.log — first N lines (oldest)\n  - tail /log/<ns>/<env>/<svc>/main.log — last N lines; tail -f follows\n  - ls /log — browse the namespace tree; ls /log/<ns>/<svc> lists a service's kinds\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mText pipeline\x1b[0m\n  - grep — filter lines by pattern\n  - awk — field-oriented extraction / predicates\n  - sed — stream substitution\n  - jq — JSON filtering and projection (implies JSON source)\n  - cut, paste — column slicing and joining\n  - sort, uniq — ordering and de-duplication\n  - wc — line/word/byte counts\n  - rev, tac — reverse chars / reverse line order\n  - tee, tr — duplicate to file / translate chars\n  - xargs — build commands from stdin\n  - less — page the final output (see man less)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFilesystem\x1b[0m\n  - ls [-l] [-a] [-h] [path] — list /bin, /cron.d, /etc, /log, /tmp; ls -l /cron.d/ adds a sparkline\n  - cat /bin/<fn> — view a saved function\n  - cat /cron.d/<name> — view a cron definition\n  - chart <cron> / ... | chart [-x COL|.path] [-y COL|.path] — render a saved cron or a pipeline as an in-terminal chart (man chart)\n  - > / >> — redirect pipeline output into /tmp\n  - mkdir /tmp/<dir> — group scratch files into folders\n  - cp <src> <dst> / mv <src> <dst> — duplicate / rename (both sides must be inside /tmp; cat <src> > <dst> writes elsewhere)\n  - touch <path> — create empty / reset the 48h clock on a /tmp file (>, >>, cp and mv reset it too)\n  - rm <path> / rm -rf <dir> — delete a file or directory\n  - vi <path> — edit (vim keys)\n  - cd <path> — change the virtual cwd\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWorking on saved results\x1b[0m\n  - diff <a> <b> — line-level differences between two files\n  - comm <a> <b> — sorted-set merge: only-in-a, only-in-b, in-both\n  - join <a> <b> — relational join on a key column (-t / -1 / -2, with a space before the value: -t ,)\n  - paste <a> <b> [...] — column-merge files line-by-line (-d)\n  - wc <files> — -l lines, -w words, -c bytes, -m chars, -L longest line\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mVersion history (git)\x1b[0m\n  git is a subset of git over /bin, /cron.d, /etc/dashboards and /etc/notify.yaml; HEAD is the org's latest change.\n\n  - git log [-p] [--oneline] [-- <path>] — list versions; -p includes diffs\n  - git show <rev>[:<path>] — a commit, or a file's content at that revision (path without a leading slash: git show HEAD:bin/<name>)\n  - git diff <a> <b> [-- <path>] — unified diff between two revisions\n  - git blame <path> — who last changed each line\n  - git restore --source=<rev> <path> — make a past version current (a new commit)\n  - git revert <rev> — undo one commit with a new commit\n  - git commit --amend -m MSG — reword the latest commit\n\n  Revisions: HEAD, HEAD~N, <a>..<b>, or a hash prefix (≥4 hex chars).\n  Tracked: /bin, /cron.d, /etc/dashboards and /etc/notify.yaml; last 5 past versions retained.\n  git status works; other git commands are refused as unsupported.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSharing\x1b[0m\n  - clipboard /bin/<name> — copy a short link to the script (current range + args) to the system clipboard; teammates open it in their shell pre-filled (man clipboard)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTeam (admins)\x1b[0m\n  - cat /etc/passwd — the team roster: each member's role, status, and groups (read-only)\n  - adduser <email> — invite someone to the org (sends an invite email)\n  - adduser <email> <group> — add a member to a group; deluser <email> <group> removes them from it\n  - deluser <email> — remove a member from the org\n  - groups <email> / id <email> — show a member's groups\n\n  Groups are the permission model: binmgr / cronmgr / configmgr\n  (edit /bin, /cron.d, /etc), nsmgr (create and manage namespaces),\n  a namespace name (read its logs), or <ns>+tokens (mint producer tokens\n  for it). Admins have implicit full access. See man passwd.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAlerting\x1b[0m\n  - <pipeline> | alert above <N> --notify=<alias> — fire a notification when the pipeline's final number exceeds N; alert must be the last command of a script. See help alerts or man alert\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mShell\x1b[0m\n  - range [<start>..<end>] — show or set the query window, e.g. range 2h..\n  - which <name> — show where a command lives\n  - type <name> — identify a command\n  - history — past commands\n  - clear — clear the screen\n  - exit — quit the shell\n\n\x1b[38;5;8mEach entry above is a command — man <name> where a page exists.\x1b[0m\n"

// HelpCron — Crons (generated from docs/shell/help-cron.md).
const HelpCron = "\x1b[38;5;208mCrons\x1b[0m\n  A cron runs a pipeline on a schedule. Its stdout is recorded\n  as the cron result; writes to stderr are treated as alert firings\n  (if the cron has a | alert stage — see help alerts).\n\n  - vi /cron.d/<name> — create or edit a cron\n  - cat /cron.d/<name> — view a cron definition\n  - ls /cron.d — list existing crons; ls -l /cron.d/ adds a sparkline column\n  - chart <name> — chart recent results (man chart)\n  - rm /cron.d/<name> — delete a cron (stops the schedule)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCron file format\x1b[0m\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  # schedule: takes every <duration> or a five-field expression such\n  as */5 * * * *. Notification targets come from the alert stage's\n  --notify=<alias>. Each run reads only the logs from its own\n  schedule interval, so count with no row limit. Do not use head -n or\n  tail -n in a cron: they can cut the interval short.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAccessing past results\x1b[0m\n  weft::latest, weft::avg, weft::max, weft::min, weft::sum,\n  weft::stddev, and the rest of the weft::* family read a cron's\n  history; pass the cron's name, e.g. weft::latest error-rate. Windows\n  are durations like 30m, 1h or 1d (aggregates reach back 24 hours).\n  The predicates (weft::is_above, weft::is_outlier, etc.) return\n  exit codes for composing alerts. Full reference: man weft-funcs.\n\n    echo $(weft::avg error-rate 1h) | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  (Here error-rate is the name of one of your own crons.)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTier limits\x1b[0m\n  {{TIER_LIMITS}}\n"

